<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Clean Water Is Important Because...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Summarize a short story or text. Interactive: Participate in collaborative story-writing with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can summarize the main points of a text on water importance (e.g., "Water is essential for people's health and plants because it helps them grow and stay alive.") Can contribute sentences in a collaborative story about water conservation (e.g., "The family saved water every day.") Can collaborate in a group to write ideas on water usage and conservation (e.g., "We use water for cooking and cleaning.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large image of a flowing river and a dry cracked earth. Students work in pairs to brainstorm two sentences describing each image using vocabulary like 'water', 'resource', 'drought', 'shortage', 'available', 'limited'. The teacher asks pairs to share one sentence from each image, focusing on simple written output.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher writes a sentence on the board: "If we waste water, there will be a shortage." The teacher models writing another sentence: "We use water for drinking, cooking, and cleaning because it's essential." The teacher highlights the grammar structures (first conditional, for + -ing, because) and checks for clear handwriting and sentence capitalization. Pronunciation of "shortage" and "essential" is briefly modeled, emphasizing stress patterns for compound words and syllabification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What happens if we don't save water?" (Elicit "We will have a shortage.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do we use water *for*?" (Elicit "For drinking, for cleaning.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Importance of Water")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short informational paragraph (approx. 60-80 words) titled "Why Water is Our Most Important Resource," using target vocabulary (potable, essential, limited, conserve, daily, filter, sanitation) and grammar structures (because, for + -ing).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Text: "Water is our most essential resource because we use it daily for drinking, cooking, and cleaning. It is important for our health and for sanitation. If we waste water, it will become limited. We must conserve this potable resource wisely. Some communities need to filter their water to make it drinkable."</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading and understanding short written texts about environmental issues.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> What is the main idea of this text? (Students write down one sentence.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read sentences based on the text and write "True" or "False". (e.g., "Water is important only for drinking." - False).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Match words from the text (e.g., "conserve," "potable") to their simple definitions.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences using the first conditional, "for + verb + ing" to express purpose, and "because" to express cause-effect, incorporating target vocabulary related to water importance and conservation (save, waste, shortage, essential, conserve, limited, daily).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides sentence beginnings and students complete them using the target grammar and vocabulary. Example: "If we don't save water,..." (students write "we will face a shortage."). "We use water for..." (students write "drinking and cleaning."). "Water is important because..." (students write "it is an essential resource.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Sentence Combination:</b> Students are given two short sentences and combine them using "because" or "if...then..." Example: "Water is limited. We must conserve it." (Students write: "We must conserve water because it is limited."). "You turn off the tap. You save water." (Students write: "If you turn off the tap, you will save water.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Construction:</b> In small groups (3-4 students), students write three sentences about how their family uses water daily, and three sentences about how they save water. They must use 'for + -ing' in the first set and 'if... will...' or 'because' in the second set. Example: "My family uses water for cooking and washing clothes daily." "If we take short showers, we will save a lot of water." Groups then peer-check each other's sentences for correct grammar and vocabulary usage.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Importance of Water")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Collaborative Story Writing & Summarization.</b> Students work in groups of 3-4 to perform two writing tasks:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Collaborative Story:</b> Each group creates a short story (5-7 sentences) about a family or a community that faces a water shortage and learns to conserve water. Students must incorporate at least two "If...then..." sentences, two "for + -ing" clauses, and two "because" clauses. Vocabulary such as *shortage, conserve, tap, waste, collect, daily, wisely* should be used. The teacher encourages discussion about solutions to environmental issues during brainstorming (sociolinguistic competence).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Summarization:</b> After writing their story, each group writes a 2-3 sentence summary of their own story, focusing on the main message of water conservation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme & Scenario:</b> This activity directly addresses the theme "Clean Water Is Important Because..." by having students create narratives and summaries that highlight water usage, conservation, and the consequences of misuse. It provides an authentic context for expressing cause-effect and purpose through writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in written expression, collaborative interaction for story development, and successful completion of a communicative writing task that demonstrates understanding of water importance and conservation strategies.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Group Writing Rubric & Peer Assessment.</b> The teacher uses a simple rubric to evaluate group work and written output. Groups also conduct a peer assessment of another group's story.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group submits their collaborative story and its summary. Peer groups review the stories for clarity, grammar, and vocabulary usage, providing constructive feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Rubric & Peer Assessment Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target grammar (First conditional, for + -ing, because).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (at least 5 relevant words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the story.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Completeness of the summary (captures main points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of collaboration during writing.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick reflection sheet:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I used today in my writing is ________."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I found writing sentences with 'if...will...' (easy/difficult) because ________."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing our story was the most fun or interesting?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing you learned about saving water today, and how can you write about it?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative story writing allowed students to actively apply the target grammar and vocabulary in a meaningful context. The peer-checking enhanced their awareness of accuracy. Some students struggled with consistently forming grammatically correct 'if' clauses, indicating a need for more explicit practice or sentence frames. Adjustments for the next lesson could include providing more structured sentence starters for complex grammatical patterns if needed, and encouraging students to review their written work for pronunciation of stressed words (e.g., 'important' vs. 'importantly').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This writing lesson prepares students for Lesson 5, where they will mediate and integrate information about water conservation. Their ability to summarize and collaboratively write will be crucial for the 21st Century Skills Project, which may involve creating a public awareness poster or presentation based on the learned information and stories.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>